  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 Days...\n\nOur main weather maker remains the pesky upper level low that is\nnow slowly progressing eastwards into the Canadian Prairies. \nSouth of this feature, into our area, 700mb wind speeds out of the\nwest are widespread in the 30 kts range. With model soundings \nshowing that the the entire region as already fully mixed out to \nnearly 600mb, these winds are having no trouble reaching the \nsurface. In the foothills of south central Montana this setup \nseems almost like what we would see in the fall as the winds are \nperfectly oriented to gap between the terrin. Locations along the\nUS-191 corridor (Harlowton and Big Timber) could see winds gusts \nin the 50s mph rage this afternoon. The greatest chance for the \nstrongest gusts will be just ahead of a lobe of energy that is \nforecast to pivot around the main feature and drive out of the AB\nRockies into N. WY. Winds at the 700mb layer could be over 50kts \nstarting roughly around 00z this evening. With this push of \nstronger winds however, the flow will shift to more northwesterly \nthus reducing the flow between most mountain gaps. The one \nexception is that this could give the Judith Gap area a potential to\nstart gusting into the 50s mph. \n\nWhile some locations overnight tonight may see an ease in winds,\nmainly west of Billings due to terrain, the general consensus \namongst models is that breezy conditions will continue overnight. \nThe length of daylight this time of year only allows for a short \nperiod of time where the PBL can truly decouple and keep the \nwinds aloft. Additionally, as this vort max moves through the \nregion an increase in cloud cover will help keep overnight lows \nmodest. Rain showers could also help mix down any of the stronger\nwinds aloft as well. \n\nSpeaking of the precip, there are now in increase in radar \nreturns over the western half of the CWA at this hour. Over the \ncoming hours, as the large area of lift ahead of the vort max \npushes farther into our CWA, expect these showers to become more \nwidespread, especially over south-centeral Montana. The one \nexception to this is that due to the strong westerly winds, \ndownsloping will likely play a significant role in limiting the \nprecipitation for locations west of Billings. That being said, \nshowers and thunderstorms are still expected. \n\nAlthough none of these thunderstorms are forecast to be\nsevere, a stray strong downburst may produce locally strong \nwinds. With the aforementioned steep lapse rates in the low to \nmid levels, D-Cape currently analyzed near 500 J/Kg, and little \nwinds sheer at all, the strongest updrafts of the day may be able \nto collapse with just enough force for a very localized event. \nAlso with freezing levels around 7kft, any storm witha decent \nupdraft wil be capable of producing small hail. \n\nLocations west of Billings will see 700mb temps at or below 0C for\nmuch of the event, even locally dipping down to -5C overnight.\nThis will keep snow levels roughly around 7kft MSL meaning that \naccumulating snow is forecast for the high terrin, including the \nBeartooth Pass. \n\nLooking ahead, while the parent upper level low does drift east\ninto northern Ontario Friday, there will still be lobes of energy\nassociated with it that move through our area. While widespread \nprecipitation in not expected, chances for scattered showers and \nthunderstorms will continue though the weekend. No severe weather \nis expected. \n\nOnce this feature finally moves eastwards enough there is a \nsignal that some weak ridging could build in for the start of the\nnext work week with warm temperatures and dry conditions \nreturning. Do not get too used to this however, because another \nwave of energy could move through mid to late next week brining \nmore chances for showers and cooler weather. WMR",
